  "id": "Compound::DB00381 - Gene::776",
  "verbalization": "Amlodipine, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, is consistently linked to Cacna1d through multiple pathways. The core mechanism involves Amlodipine binding to Cacna1b, which is a subunit of the high voltage-gated calcium channel. This binding modulates calcium channel activity, and since Cacna1b is part of this activity, it is likely that Amlodipine influences Cacna1d through a similar mechanism. The evidence is further supported by the inclusion of Amlodipine in the class of dihydropyridines, which includes Clevidipine and Felodipine, all of which bind to Cacna1d. This suggests a broader pharmacological relationship where dihydropyridine agents interact with the calcium channel subunit Cacna1d. The integration of these pathways highlights a mechanistic link between Amlodipine and Cacna1d, mediated through calcium channel modulation. The evidence is robust, with multiple independent pathways converging on the same core mechanism, reinforcing the hypothesis that Amlodipine exerts its effects on Cacna1d via calcium channel inhibition.",
